  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 PM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nRAP surface analysis this afternoon shows sprawling high pressure \nfrom the Lower Mississippi River Valley reaching north to Hudson \nBay. The tight E-W pressure gradient which instigated this past\nweek's LES is shown departing the region as the overhead \npattern becomes increasingly barotropic. Visible satellite \nimagery shows lingering lake effect clouds over the eastern half\nof the UP, with the west under nearly clear skies; temperatures\ncurrently sit in the upper 30s to mid 40s. Aloft, the mid-\nlevels show a skinny omega pattern across CONUS, with a wedge of\nhigh pressure building northward through the Plains. Water \nvapor imagery shows an area of deep subsidence pressing downward\ninto the Upper Great Lakes.\n\nAs the high progresses slowly eastward, northerly surface flow and \nCAA persists into tonight as we remain on the leeward side of the \nhigh's anticyclonic flow. Winds back to southwesterly throughout \ntomorrow, and temperatures in the meantime remain relatively close \nto normal. Cloud cover today and tomorrow contributes to highs 5-10F \nabove normal in the 40s today and tomorrow, while overnight lows \nthrough Sunday land 0-10F above normal in the 20s and low 30s both \nnights. With the change in wind direction, lake effect clouds \ndiminish Sunday afternoon, and temperatures rebound to the 50s (15-\n20F above normal) Monday through Wednesday. While this is somewhat \nhighlighted by the current run of the ECMWF's EFI table for this \ntime period, the NBM currently shows greater than 95% probability of \nMaxT >50F for Tuesday and Wednesday (same for >48F for Monday). This \nwarm-up is accompanied by clear skies and dry weather through \nTuesday, though clouds increase once again starting Wednesday.\n\nNext chances for precipitation arrive late Wednesday into Thursday \nas a Colorado low lifts out of the Plains. As of now, broad \nagreement exists amongst ensembles that some precipitation will \noccur throughout Thursday, though current guidance indicates it's \nunlikely to be very impactful as LREF 24-hour precipitation \ndistributions range from 0\" to 0.15\" for this system. As far as\np- type goes, ensembles also agree that rain is most likely to \nfall Thursday. This is in alignment with anomalously high \ntemperatures forecast for the late week period, supported by EFI\nSoT values of ~0.77 for minimum temperatures Wednesday through \nFriday nights, lending weight to above-freezing conditions these\nnights. Details will continue to emerge and solidify in the \nmeantime.\n\nInto next weekend, a second, deeper Colorado low brings higher \nchances of widespread precipitation. This one is literally and \nfiguratively all over the map, with ensemble members showing decent \nspread in timing, track, and p-type. For now, they do agree on \nwidespread precipitation Saturday into Sunday, with more likely rain \non Saturday and some snow potential on Sunday.",
